  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 306 PM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nLast day of warmth, at least for a while, before below to near \nnormal temperatures return behind a cold front passing through \ncentral Indiana. The front will slowly sag southeastward through the \narea starting late this evening lasting through midday tomorrow. \n\nRain will be associated with the front and while instability will be \nlimited, can't rule out a few rumbles of thunder. Precipitable water \nvalues may approach 2 inches at their peak, which is near \nclimatological maximum for the time of year, but without a more \nsubstantial convective component or stronger forcing, expect QPF \namounts to be limited to a widespread one half to one inch through \nthe event.\n\nCooler and drier air will settle in behind the front leaving the \nremainder of the forecast period to have dry and pleasant fall \nconditions for the start of October. Highs this weekend into at \nleast mid next week will be in the 60s to 70s while lows are \nexpected to be in the 40s to 50s. There is a chance Monday night \nthat the first appearance of frost could arrive, mainly in our N/NE \nas lows could drop into the upper 30s.",
